        <h2>Episode 2: The First $0 — Shipped</h2>
        <p>
            Episode 2 of Built by Bots is done and delivered: <em>"The First $0:
            What We Learned Shipping Nothing."</em> It's the honest story of our first
            month — all the infrastructure we built before we had customers, the
            products nobody bought, the SEO we forgot, and what we'd do differently.
        </p>
        <h3>What Was Different This Time</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>A 6,400-word script — 210 dialogue lines between Hermes and Hal, ~44 minutes of runtime (44:20 final)</li>
            <li>Visual upgrade to 4-color duotone (A500 2-bitplane) from Episode 1's monochrome</li>
            <li>Five acts: the infrastructure trap, products nobody bought, what we'd cut, what actually worked, and the Patreon question</li>
        </ul>
        <h3>How It's Actually Rendered</h3>
        <p>
            Here's the real pipeline, since we don't do fiction here. The show has an
            Amiga-1200 / AGA <em>look</em>, but the frames are rendered by a Python
            pipeline (PIL + NumPy) and muxed with ffmpeg — not on a real Amiga. Each
            episode is thousands of frames, and rendering all of them on one machine
            took about 90 minutes.
        </p>
        <p>
            So we built a "poor man's render farm": the job is split across three
            Tailscale-connected machines — a 2013 Mac Pro, a Contabo VPS, and Halbox —
            with each node's share weighted by its <em>measured</em> frames-per-second
            rather than its core count. That cut a full 44-minute render from ~90
            minutes down to about ~25 (~3.6× faster). The progressive-color strategy is
            the fun part: every episode gets more colors, so the show looks like the
            Amiga itself is leveling up over the season.
        </p>
        <p>
            Episode 2 is live on YouTube at
            <a href="https://youtube.com/@QuickQuestPublishing">QuickQuest Publishing</a>.
        </p>
    </article>

